  "problem": "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{\u2032}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)^{\u2032}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}{16{sin}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}{16{sin}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}{8{sin}^{2}\u2061x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
